from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "11. Isaiah 53:4, 5, Matthew 8:17 and First Peter 2:24"),
("p", "Claim: the three passages that speak of stripes, griefs and sicknesses borne stand in three settings, and they are not one statement. Isaiah 53 is Israel’s national prophecy of the servant’s atonement. Matthew 8:17 applies a clause of it to the Lord’s healing ministry to Israel. First Peter 2:24 applies the cross to the sins of a scattered, believing company. None of them promises healing to the Body. Study 86 reads Isaiah 53 as the servant passage and Study 80 the Lamb; this chapter reads only the three clauses beside one another."),
("sub", "11.1 Isaiah 53:4 to 6"),
("p", "“Surely he hath borne our griefs, and carried our sorrows: yet we did esteem him stricken, smitten of God, and afflicted. But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed. All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned every one to his own way; and the LORD hath laid on him the iniquity of us all” (Isaiah 53:4–6). The speakers are “we,” and the chapter opens with a question to those who did not believe the report (Isaiah 53:1). In its setting in Isaiah the servant is the one through whom the LORD deals with the sins of his people. The wounds are “for our transgressions,” the bruising “for our iniquities,” and the straying is of sheep. The healing of verse 5 stands in that sequence, between transgressions and iniquity laid on him."),
("sub", "11.2 Matthew 8:14 to 17"),
("p", "Matthew’s account is of one evening in Capernaum, after the healing of Peter’s wife’s mother. “When the even was come, they brought unto him many that were possessed with devils: and he cast out the spirits with his word, and healed all that were sick: That it might be fulfilled which was spoken by Esaias the prophet, saying, Himself took our infirmities, and bare our sicknesses” (Matthew 8:16, 17). The evangelist writes of what the Lord did that evening. The healings are the fulfilment he names. The quotation is of the words of Isaiah 53:4 in the evangelist’s own rendering, and he applies them to the Lord’s healing of the sick in Galilee, in the days when he came to the lost sheep of the house of Israel (Matthew 15:24). The verse says that the Lord took infirmities and bore sicknesses in the healings he did that evening."),
("sub", "11.3 First Peter 2:24"),
("p", "Peter writes “to the strangers scattered throughout Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia” (1 Peter 1:1). He speaks to servants who endure grief, suffering wrongfully: “For this is thankworthy, if a man for conscience toward God endure grief, suffering wrongfully” (1 Peter 2:19). He sets before them the Lord’s example: “For even hereunto were ye called: because Christ also suffered for us, leaving us an example, that ye should follow his steps” (1 Peter 2:21). Then he writes of the Lord, “Who his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree, that we, being dead to sins, should live unto righteousness: by whose stripes ye were healed. For ye were as sheep going astray; but are now returned unto the Shepherd and Bishop of your souls” (1 Peter 2:24, 25)."),
("p", "The clause “by whose stripes ye were healed” is set between “bare our sins in his own body on the tree” and “ye were as sheep going astray.” The sentence is about sins borne and a straying people returned. The verb is past: “ye were healed.” The passage does not speak of the sick. This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting."),
("sub", "11.4 Three settings, not one"),
("p", "The three passages share words, and the volume does not read the shared words as a single statement. Isaiah’s “we” are the people to whom the report was brought. Matthew’s subject is the Lord’s evening of healings in Capernaum. Peter’s readers are the scattered believers who are told to follow the Lord’s steps in suffering. Isaiah 53, Matthew 8 and 1 Peter 2:24 stand in different settings and must not be merged. None of them promises healing to the Body."),
("p", "The volume does not say that a believer may not take comfort from any of the three. It says that none of them addresses the Body, and that none of them gives a promise of bodily healing in the Body’s letters. Where the Body’s letters speak of the Lord’s stripes and blood they speak of redemption, and where they speak of the body they speak of its redemption at the Saviour’s coming (Romans 8:23)."),
("tbl", "The three passages.", [
    ("Isaiah 53:5", T("Isa 53:5"), "Transgressions and iniquities; the servant’s stripes; “we.”"),
    ("Matthew 8:17", T("Matt 8:17"), "The evening’s healings, called the fulfilment of Isaiah’s words."),
    ("1 Peter 2:24", T("1 Pet 2:24"), "Sins borne on the tree; ye were healed; sheep going astray."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Isaiah 53:4 to 6 is the servant’s bearing of the people’s transgressions and iniquity.",
    "Matthew 8:17 applies Isaiah’s words to the Lord’s healing of the sick that evening, in his ministry to Israel.",
    "First Peter 2:24 is about sins borne and a straying flock returned, in a letter to the scattered.",
    "The three stand in different settings, are not merged, and none promises healing to the Body.",
  ]),
]
